Q: Detect dynamically allocated object? A: <p>Very interesting topic. I can see two portable ways in solving the management of the object.</p>
<p><strong>The first one</strong> is to create a class with a single pointer as a member data, and to always manage the class through that pointer. So there will be no difference, whether the class is created dynamically, locally, statically, globally etc. The class could have a copy constructor, which will allow to pass it as an argument. The class data could be declared as internal private structure, then the object creation, management and deletion will be done using that structure. The pointer to that structure (a single data member) could be opaque (of <code>void*</code> type), which is very common technique using object handlers. The derived classes must have then their own mechanism to handle their additional data.</p>
<p><strong>The second solution</strong> is to overload the <code>new</code> and <code>new[]</code> operators within the class, as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/596781/kerrek-sb">Kerrek SB</a> said. This works perfectly with base and derived classes. The overloaded operator functions have to set internal static thread-safe allocation flag. This allows to determine in the called right away class constructor, whether or not the overloaded function was called. The class constructor copies its value into a member flag, and reset the static flag. Calling the <code>new[]</code> operator will set allocation flag only in the first object in the array, which is great, since only the pointer to the first object could be freed. The static flag must be <code>false</code> at every allocation call, so the mismanagement can be easily caught.</p>
<p>Here is working portable example. It could be improved with class allocation mismanagement handling, bad allocation handling, exceptions etc.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;
#include &lt;cstring&gt;

// sets allocation flag in the constructor
class MyClass
{
public:
    int m_Data[9]; // other data
    MyClass()
    {
        // set member flag and reset static flag
        m_Allocated = s_Allocated;
        s_Allocated = false;

        // initialize class data
        std::memset(&amp;m_Data, 0, sizeof(m_Data));
    }
    bool IsAllocated() const { return m_Allocated; }
    static void* operator new(size_t size) { return alloc_class(size); }
    static void* operator new[](size_t size) { return alloc_class(size); }

private:
    bool m_Allocated; // member flag
    static thread_local bool s_Allocated; // thread-safe static flag
    static void* alloc_class(size_t size)
    {
        if (s_Allocated != false) { /*... alloc mismanagement ...*/ }
        s_Allocated = true; // set allocation flag
        void* ptr = std::malloc(size);
        if (!ptr) { /*... bad alloc handling ...*/ }
        return ptr;
    }
};

// thread-safe static flag definition
thread_local bool MyClass::s_Allocated = false;

int main()
{
    MyClass mc1; // false
    MyClass* pmc1 = new MyClass; // true

    MyClass mc2[3]; // false in every
    MyClass* pmc2 = new MyClass[3]; // true in first

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Handling silently-returning methods: overloads, parameters, or other? A: <p>I'd switch it around. If I'm reading API docs/ scrolling through IntelliSense suggestions and I see a method <code>Search</code> I'd assume that that method actually searches, so I'd switch it like</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public void SearchIfNewModel(SearchModel searchModel)
{
    if (SearchModelEqualityComparer.Equals(_lastSearchModel, searchModel))
        return;

    Search(searchModel);
}

public void Search(SearchModel searchModel) =&gt; ...
</code></pre>
<p>(I'd even argue that <code>SearchIfNewModel</code> should return a boolean indicating whether it actually searched or didn't)</p>
